WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.119
Hello. Forcing a model to call a tool only gets you halfway. The call still has to carry the right

2
00:00:07.119 --> 00:00:13.760
inputs, in the right types, with nothing missing. In this lesson, we'll go through strict tool use,

3
00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:19.280
a single setting that guarantees the model's tool inputs match the schema you defined.

4
00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:23.200
Let's get into it. Let's start with the pain it removes.

5
00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:27.040
Without strict mode, the model can hand your function the wrong shape of data.

6
00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:31.360
Say your schema expects a number of passengers. You might get back the word

7
00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:36.639
two spelled out as a string. Or you might get a two wrapped in quotes, which is a string,

8
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.159
not the integer your code wants. Worse, the model might leave out a field you marked as

9
00:00:42.159 --> 00:00:46.400
required entirely. Every one of those breaks the function you're trying to call.

10
00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.959
And the usual fix is ugly. You write code that inspects the tool call,

11
00:00:50.959 --> 00:00:55.680
checks whether the types are right, and if they're not, you re-prompt the model and hope

12
00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:01.599
the second try is cleaner. That validate and retry dance is exactly what strict mode is designed

13
00:01:01.599 --> 00:01:09.599
to end. So, what does strict actually promise you? Three things. First, a valid name. The

14
00:01:09.599 --> 00:01:15.120
tool name the model returns is always a real tool from your request. It can't invent one that

15
00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:21.839
doesn't exist. Second, no type mismatch. Every argument matches the type your schema declared.

16
00:01:21.839 --> 00:01:25.760
If you said integer, you get an integer. Never a string standing in for one.

17
00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:32.879
Third, no missing fields. Every required field is present. Nothing your function depends on

18
00:01:32.879 --> 00:01:39.199
quietly goes missing. Put those together and you have a hard guarantee. The tool call conforms to

19
00:01:39.199 --> 00:01:44.800
your input schema every time by construction. You're not hoping the model got it right.

20
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:48.319
The system makes it impossible for the call to come out any other way.

21
00:01:49.279 --> 00:01:55.040
Now, let's look under the hood because the how is genuinely clever. You enable it by adding one

22
00:01:55.040 --> 00:02:00.959
property to your tool. You set strict to true right alongside the name, the description, and

23
00:02:00.959 --> 00:02:07.760
the input schema. Every object in your schema also needs additional properties set to false,

24
00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:13.759
and leaving that out gets you a 400 from the API. When you do that, the API takes your schema and

25
00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:20.080
compiles it into a grammar. That grammar then constrains the model's token sampling as it writes

26
00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:26.479
the tool call. At every step, the model can only produce tokens that keep the output valid against

27
00:02:26.479 --> 00:02:31.600
your schema. Anything that would violate it simply can't be sampled. It's called constrained

28
00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:38.399
decoding, and it happens live as the model generates, not as a check afterward. Here's my

29
00:02:38.399 --> 00:02:43.440
favorite part. What you get to stop writing. Because the guarantee holds by construction,

30
00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:49.119
you get no validation layer. That's the code that used to inspect every tool call and confirm the

31
00:02:49.119 --> 00:02:55.279
types and fields were correct. Gone. You also get no retry loop, the logic that re-prompted the

32
00:02:55.279 --> 00:03:02.160
model whenever a call came back malformed. Also gone. What's left gives you type safe calls.

33
00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:07.039
Your functions receive correctly typed arguments every single time, which means they behave

34
00:03:07.039 --> 00:03:12.960
predictably even when you're running at real production scale. Less code, fewer failure paths,

35
00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:18.559
more confidence. That's a genuinely good trade, and it's the practical reason Teams turns strict

36
00:03:18.559 --> 00:03:24.880
on for agent work. Now I want to draw a clear line between two features that sound similar,

37
00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:30.240
because students mix them up constantly. Strict tool use constrains the tool inputs.

38
00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:35.360
It makes sure the arguments going into your functions conform to your input schema. It's

39
00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:40.880
about what flows into the tools. Structured outputs, on the other hand, constrains the text

40
00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:47.119
response itself. It shapes the model's final answer to a schema you provide. It's about the shape of

41
00:03:47.119 --> 00:03:54.080
what comes back to the user or the calling code. Same underlying idea, valid by construction,

42
00:03:54.080 --> 00:04:00.639
pointed at two different targets. One governs the tool call inputs, the other governs the response.

43
00:04:00.639 --> 00:04:04.639
Keep that distinction sharp and you'll always know which one you actually need.

44
00:04:05.839 --> 00:04:11.679
Here's what ties them together under the surface. Strict tool use compiles your schemas into

45
00:04:11.679 --> 00:04:17.040
grammars using the very same pipeline that structured outputs uses. It's not a separate

46
00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:24.000
system bolted on, it's the same machinery aimed at tool inputs. Because of that shared foundation,

47
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:30.239
both features accept the same supported subset of JSON schema. That matters in practice. A

48
00:04:30.239 --> 00:04:35.279
handful of the more advanced schema keywords aren't allowed, so you keep your schemas within

49
00:04:35.279 --> 00:04:41.119
the supported subset. And there's a performance nicety. Those compiled schemas get cached for up

50
00:04:41.119 --> 00:04:48.000
to 24 hours since their last use. So if you call the same tool repeatedly, you skip the recompilation

51
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:55.679
cost after the first time. Same pipeline, same schema rules, same cache. One more thing worth

52
00:04:55.679 --> 00:05:01.359
calling out because it connects straight back to how you steer tool selection. Strict combines

53
00:05:01.359 --> 00:05:07.119
cleanly with the tool choice settings we looked at earlier. Set tool choice to any and you guarantee

54
00:05:07.119 --> 00:05:12.559
that some tool gets called. Then set strict to true on that tool and you guarantee the inputs

55
00:05:12.559 --> 00:05:17.760
to it are valid. Stack those two together and you've closed the loop. You're certain a call

56
00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:23.119
happens and you're certain the call is well formed. There's no gap left where the model could either

57
00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:28.480
skip the tool or feed it garbage. For anyone building agent workflows that have to run

58
00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:34.480
unattended, that combination is a genuinely strong foundation to build on. Reliable trigger,

59
00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:41.040
reliable payload. Strict tool use gives you a different way to think about tool inputs.

60
00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:46.079
Conformance stops being something you check after the fact and becomes something that's true

61
00:05:46.079 --> 00:05:53.200
by construction. What makes this work is the decoding itself because the model can't emit a

62
00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:59.359
token that would break your schema. So the real payoff isn't fewer malformed calls. It's the

63
00:05:59.359 --> 00:06:04.160
validation layer and the retry loops that you don't have to write at all because the process

64
00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:08.640
is guaranteed. That's where we'll leave this one. I'll see you in the next lesson.

